Scott's Exercise 5.10 asks, for neighbourhood systems \(\mathcal{D}_0\) and \(\mathcal{D}_1\) on disjoint token sets, to form the smash \(\mathcal{D}_0\otimes\mathcal{D}_1\) whose neighbourhoods are the union of the two masters together with unions \(X\cup Y\) of proper neighbourhoods, to prove that this is a neighbourhood system, to define \(\mathcal{D}_0\to_\bot\mathcal{D}_1\) so that its elements are exactly the strict maps, and, by combinators, to show that the elements of \(\mathcal{D}_0\to_\bot(\mathcal{D}_1\to_\bot\mathcal{D}_2)\) and of \((\mathcal{D}_0\otimes\mathcal{D}_1)\to_\bot\mathcal{D}_2\) are isomorphic. The disjoint carriers are the sum type and `prodNbhd` is the tagged union. `smash` takes as neighbourhoods the product of the two masters or a `SmashProper` product of two non-master neighbourhoods, with that product of masters as its own master (`smash_master`, `smash_mem_iff`). The neighbourhood axioms are the construction of `smash`: a master factor is absorbed because every neighbourhood lies under its master, and if both factors are proper a master witness would force a factor to equal its master, so the witness is a proper product whose factors lie in the two intersections, which remain proper by `inter_ne_master_left` and `inter_ne_master_right`. Strictness is `IsStrict`, relating the master input only to the master output, and `isStrict_iff_apply_bot` is the element form \(f(\bot)=\bot\). `strictFun` is the neighbourhood system of strict maps whose neighbourhoods are the nonempty step-function sets of finite lists of neighbourhood pairs (`sstep`, `sstepFun`); the empty list is every strict map (`sstepFun_nil`) and contains the constantly bottom map (`isStrict_constBot`), and a consistent intersection concatenates the lists (`sstepFun_append`, `strictFun_mem_inter`). `strictFunEquiv` is the order isomorphism between elements and strict maps: `toStrictMap` reads a filter as the map relating a pair when its step lies in the filter, `toStrictFilter` collects the neighbourhoods containing a strict map, and the round trips compare `mem_sstepFun_iff` with `sstep_mem_of_mem`. The combinators are `smashCurryMap` and `smashUncurryMap`. Curry sends a strict map out of the smash to the map whose value at a neighbourhood \(X\) is the strict section `smashSection`, the monotone extension of \(Y\mapsto g(\langle\uparrow X,\uparrow Y\rangle_\otimes)\); at a master input `smashPair_bot_left` collapses the pairing and strictness forces every output in the step list to be the master, so the output neighbourhood is the whole strict function space. Uncurry relates a smash neighbourhood \(W\) to an output \(Z\) exactly when the original map relates the left preimage of \(W\) to the step of the right preimage and \(Z\); on the smash master that step is everything (`sstep_master_eq`), and the constantly bottom map in the step forces the output to be the master. `section_uncurry_rel` is the identity \(g(\langle x,y\rangle_\otimes)=(\mathrm{curry}\,g)(x)(y)\) at neighbourhoods: a master factor collapses by `smashPair_bot_left` or `smashPair_bot_right`, and two proper factors return through `smashPair_principal_apply`. `smashUncurry_curry` and `smashCurry_uncurry` are the round trips, and `smashCurryEquiv` is the order isomorphism of those strict-map carriers; conjugating by `strictFunEquiv` is the isomorphism of element sets. `smashPair_eq_bot_iff` records that the strict pairing is bottom when either argument is, which the exercise does not ask as a separate clause. The numbered theorem `example_510` is only `isStrict_idMap`. **Theorem:** partial — the smash, the identification of the elements of \(\to_\bot\) with the strict maps, and the curry/uncurry isomorphism are `smash`, `strictFunEquiv`, and `smashCurryEquiv`, while `example_510` asserts only that the identity is strict. **Proof:** partial — the intersection case split, the filter representation, and the section computation with the two round trips prove the three clauses, and the proof of `example_510` is the identity argument that a master input related by the identity is the master output.
